                <p>These costs typically do not include translation services if additional document translation is needed, or courier fees if physical records need to be mailed. Payment methods vary &#x2014; many hospitals accept international credit cards, wire transfers, or payment through third-party medical tourism platforms.</p>

                <h3>Platforms Used for Telemedicine</h3>

                <p><strong>WeChat Video:</strong> The most common platform for consultations with Chinese hospitals. Your medical coordinator will typically invite you to a WeChat group that includes your surgeon and/or the international patient team. WeChat video calls are generally reliable and widely available internationally. Download WeChat and ensure you have a stable account before leaving China.</p>

                <p><strong>Hospital-Operated Telemedicine Platforms:</strong> Some hospitals have their own dedicated telemedicine systems that offer higher quality video and integrate directly with the hospital's medical records. These platforms may require you to create an account before your first consultation and could involve downloading specific applications.</p>

                <p><strong>Zoom or Microsoft Teams:</strong> For more formal consultations, some hospitals use mainstream Western video platforms. These generally offer excellent video quality and are familiar to most international patients.</p>

                <p>Test your chosen platform and internet connection before the scheduled appointment time. Ensure your device is fully charged or plugged in, and have a backup plan (phone number to call, alternative app to use) in case of technical difficulties.</p>

                <h3>When to Request a Telemedicine Consultation</h3>

                <p>Certain situations particularly benefit from a telemedicine consultation with your Chinese medical team.</p>

                <p><strong>After receiving scan results:</strong> When you have imaging performed locally and receive results that raise questions, a telemedicine consultation allows your Chinese surgeon to review the images directly and provide interpretation in the context of your surgical history. Local radiologists may miss subtle findings relevant to your specific condition if they lack context.</p>

                <p><strong>Before starting new medication:</strong> If your home doctor wants to add a new medication, checking with your Chinese team first can prevent drug interactions or conflicts with your treatment protocol. Some medications may interfere with cancer treatments, anticoagulation regimens, or other therapies you are receiving.</p>

                <p><strong>If recovery is not progressing as expected:</strong> When you feel that your recovery is slower than anticipated, or when you experience symptoms that concern you, a telemedicine consultation provides expert assessment of whether your progress is actually abnormal or simply within normal variation. This prevents both unnecessary worry and delayed intervention for genuine problems.</p>

                <p><strong>Before major decisions about your care:</strong> If your home doctors recommend additional procedures, surgery, or significant changes to your treatment plan, a telemedicine consultation ensures your Chinese treatment team has input before you proceed. They may offer perspectives or alternatives that your local physicians are not aware of.</p>
